README othello

OVERVIEW:
    Othello has 10 Java Classes and 1 interface (not including Constants or App),
    and they interact in the following ways:
        - PaneOrganizer manages and adds/removes all graphical elements of the screen.
        PaneOrganizer instantiates Game in it's constructor. It also passes gamePane
        into the game, which allows elements to be added without giving game access
        to PaneOrganizer.
        - The controls class sets up the righthand side control panel. This is done through
        several buttons and labels that allow the player to customize their game. This
        class also generates the two players and their types based of its button selections.
        - SetUpGame handles several initial set up elements of the game.
        It sets the controls to the gamePane and instantiates the referee and
        board as instance variable. This class is in charge of assigning players from the
        controls output. It starts the actual game, resets it, and applies palettes.
        - The board class manages all logic related to the board and the Squares/pieces.
        Apart from creating the actual board, this class holds all most all board
        updating logic. This includes sandwich detection, valid move detection,
        legal moves, gameover checking, and reseting the board. The board also has a
        copy constructor. This is especially important for the minimax so it can create
        fake boards to test moves out.
        - The Square class represents all squares and pieces. Instead of using two
        arrays for the two objects, it uses a "smart" approach to represent both
        at the same time. It hands color detection, graphical changes, and piece
        generation.
        - The Piece class is a solely graphical class in charge of generating
        the pieces. It has several getters and setters so that the piece's
        colors can be flipped or switched via a palette.
        - The Referee clas handles the turn taking and player logic, acting as a sort
        of real referee. It decides who gets to go (since sometimes players need to skip).
        It starts and stops timelines for computers and allows human players to click at
        appropriate times. It also acts as an intermediary to decide if a turn is
        valid for the player to take. Furthermore, the ref updates labels for
        turn taking, score, and game over.
        - The Player interface allows both the Computer and Human players to share methods
        that may have unique functions. For example, make move is different for both classes
        because one uses mouse and the other uses a minimax.
        - HumanPlayer is the human version of the player and it implements the player
        interface. The main difference is that it uses mouse action events to take turns.
        The mouse's position is also detected to calculate where a piece needs to be placed.
        - Computer player uses a recursive method with a minimax to calculate the best
        move possible to make against there opponent. Depending on the difficulty level,
        this algorithm may think as much as three turns ahead. Using a copy constructor, it
        evaluates these moves and attempts to predict opponent's moves as well.
        - Move is a class created to hold several variables describing a move. This includes
        its position and value, it is useful for the minimax to store a lot of information in
        one class.

DESIGN CHOICES:
        - I designed my turn-taking mechanism to exist within a four part loop. First,
        the ref decides who goes using the decideMove method. Through a series of
        if statements it detects if the game is over, if a player has run out of moves,
        if it is a computer or human, etc. Then the selected player takes their turn. Using
        the interface agreement, the player performs their respective move and sends it
        to the ref to check its validity. The ref accesses several checking methods
        from board to see if the move is valid, then the board proceeds to be updated.
        This cycle continues. A design choice I would like to not is my use of recursion,
        if a player needs to skip, the current player is swapped and the method calls
        itself again, this time with a different player.
        - Although an array would be simpler, it was really fun for me to use desmos
        and generate two sin waves that perfectly correspond with the eight
        way sandwich detection method. I know that this may be sub-optimal design,
        but I couldn't resist!
        - Originally, a lot of my board updating logic was in referee, but over time
        I decided to put it into board. To make each class be distinct, I decided I would
        leave ref to have logic similar to a ref in the real world. So referee is in charge
        of calling who's turn, if its valid, who needs to skip, if its gameOver, etc.
        - A lot of my logic in the referee class exists around instance variables
        currentPlayer and nonCurrentPlayer. These two variables allow me to easily
        switch turns and skip if no turns are detected since a lot of methods within
        the class rely on checking which color the currentPlayer is.
        - getbestMove is entirely instance variable free. This allows its calls to exist
        entirely independently, with all its move and board generation and evaluation being
        contained. This helped a lot with debugging, as no outside data could interfere with
        the complex calculations that the method performs.

EXTRA CREDIT:
        - Snazzy graphics & Custom player colors: To make the board look more interesting,
        I added a series of palettes that change how each of the graphical elements with colors
        look. These palettes can be applied at anytime (although they reset the game). These
        palettes are all stored in a Constants Array, and an array in Controls slickly adds
        each one without much code. This makes the list very customizable, and more palettes
        can be easily added.

